What are the diffrent sets of data we have when making a model and how do we manipulate that data for it to be usefull

Features = The diffrent types of data --> size, cost, amout, etc
Weight = How important each feature is (learned by your model)


Data Splits 

    Training Set --> Used to fit the model (learn weights or rules) --> Largest set of data > 60% 
    Validation Set (Not always needed) --> Used to tune the model (like choosing hyperparameters)
    Test Set --> Used to evaluate how well the model generalizes (final performance)

    Generally, first the X_train, y_train, X_test, and y_test sets are made and then the X_test and y_test sets are split into X_validation, y_validation, X_test, y_test

Why data spliting is important

    Overfitting --> Model is too complex, memorizes noise in training data --> Fits every tiny zig-zag in data

        * Fits every outlier/noise into the the line
        * You have too many parameters (weights) relative to data size
        * The model is too flexible (e.g. high-degree polynomial)
        * You train too long
        * You don’t use regularization

        What is regularization 

            * Regularization is a process which punishes large weights, enocuraging the model to stay simple, helps you find how important diffrent features are to the prediction and also waht features are completly irrelevent/noise

            L2 Regularization = lambda * summmation{m, j = 1} (w^2) --> w = w with j underscore

                * Shrinks all weights smoothly toward 0
                * Prefers small, spread out weights
                * Never zeros out weights completely 
                * Gently pulls all weights towards 0 

            L1 Regularization = lambda * summmation{m, j = 1} abs(w)

                * Pushes some weights exactly to 0
                * Encourages sparse models (fewer features)
                * Can be used for feature selection

            When updating you loss function (gradient function) to account for regularization the formula becomes

            Weight(n) = 
            Weight(n) - learning-rate ((derivative-loss-function(MSE, BCE, etc) / derivative_Weight(n)) + derivative-regularization-function)


 

    UnderFitting --> Model is too simple, can’t capture patterns --> Predicts a flat line



    Just Right --> 	Model generalizes well — not too simple, not too complex --> Smooth curve, follows real trend

